d move towards  renewable energy  well the research was led by a team of  scientists at oregon state university  including dr beverly law who joins me  now  welcome to you so your study found that  the majority of the 31  vital signs set new records so how  worrying is that  after covet you know we've had several  years of being confined and changing our  habits  and it really didn't change the numbers  if things just got worse 60 percent  of those variables got worse and how  close would you say we are  to tipping points those thresholds that  once  crossed could spark big and possibly  irreversible effects  well the most worrying thing is the loss  of brazilian forest  their loss has increased even more and  they are  really the workhorse of taking carbon  dioxide out of the atmosphere they're  the natural source or natural removals  of carbon dioxide out of the atmosphere  so that's that's a worrying worrying  thing and it looks like it could be  really close to tipping points you know  some of those areas could turn into  different  vegetation types and not before us  anymore  so what do you think needs to change to  try and alter those trajectories  well we've got to stop putting it off  you know we've spent 30 years not doing  much of anything  and now we only have 10 to 30 years to  make significant changes in reducing  fossil fuel emissions  just not using carbon-based energy  sources  and that includes bioenergy so that's  the first one and then  improving or protecting those natural  removals we call them natural climate  solutions  that's the land and ocean ecosystems  so we really need to tackle that now and  i just keep seeing like the national  u.s nationally determined contributions  don't say anything about protecting  the remaining intact forests temperate  rainforests  in the country were you  personally shocked at the findings of  your own report  yeah i was really hoping that that  because of covid people stopped and  thought about how they're living and  what their behavior has been and how  they've been able to get by without much  and so i thought that would be reflected  in the numbers but it's not  it's worse dr beverly law thanks very  much indeed  you're welcome in today's other climate  news a wildfire's  path of destruction through california  has been captured by a forest  camera the footage shows flames from the  dixie wildfire spreading through trees  before engulfing the camera itself the  dixie fire has burned through an area  bigger than new york city so far  and scientists say climate change will  only make these highly destructive fires  more common several towns on the shore  of lake como have been evacuated after  torrential rain  caused landslides and flooding across  the popular tourist destination  more than 60 people have been rescued  from the floods which filled the usually  picturesque  lake with debris the extreme weather  comes just days after  hailstones the size of tennis balls  damaged nearly a hundred cars in  northern italy  the high court has agreed to hear a case  brought by environmental campaigners  against the uk  government and the oil and gas authority  environmental campaigners say  tax breaks that cover the cost of  decommissioning which is the dismantling  and clearing old infrastructure  are inconsistent with britain's climate  change goals  mckayla lodge is one of the climate  activists who brought the legal  challenge  we're definitely not asking the  government to do everything overnight  we're just asking them to actually have  their actions be in line with what they  say  if they want to say they're a climate  leader if they want to say what they  want  is a climate just future then they need  to act in the same way  the iea the international energy agency  has recently said that we can have no  new investment in oil and gas if we are  to stay below 1.5 degrees of warming and  prevent  complete climate collapse and the  finalists for this year's welcome  photography prize have been announced  the prize includes a category health in  a heating world which explores how the  rapidly changing climate  can bring medical challenges the  category finalist  zakir hussein chowdhury's entry the  climate cost  shows a man salvaging his belongings  from the wreckage of his house  after cyclone anfan hit bangladesh  the crucial cop 26 climate summit  negotiations in november  could be under threat because of unequal  access to vaccines  the chair of the least developed  countries group told sky news those  most affected by climate change are at  risk of not being included in the  discussions well our climate reporter  victoria seabrook broke this story she's  here with me now so tell us more  victoria  yes so this week i've been speaking to  global figures that represent  46 countries and 1500 ngos around the  world  at cop26 these crucial climate  negotiations happening in glasgow in  november  and what they're saying is that  unequitable unfair access to the vaccine  is a threat to these negotiations  now the uk as hosts has said it's going  to try and vaccinate  anyone coming to glasgow who wouldn't  otherwise have access to the vaccine  but these organizations these leading  global figures are saying  that's sort of not the point because the  pandemic is still raging  globally we'll need 11 billion doses of  the vaccine  to end it for good and these talks  they're all about trust  and countries coming to the table and  saying yes we'll make big pledges  to cut our emissions and tackle climate  change in good faith that other  countries will do the same  so poorer nations are saying why should  we make these big pledges  when by the way we've done the least to  cause climate change in the first place  if you rich countries aren't playing  ball when it comes to access to the  vaccine  and there's also a moral question about  whether people like diplomats  although doing very important jobs  should be able to jump the cue ahead of  the vulnerable  in their population when it comes to  accessing the covered 19 vaccine  victoria thanks very much indeed now 90  projects across the uk have been awarded  government grants aimed at supporting  green  jobs and boosting nature recovery the  initiatives will each receive a share of  40 million pounds and range from  tree planting projects to restoring  wetlands  chester zoo will receive nearly one  million pounds to create  a nature recovery corridor we want to  connect  uh habitats for wildlife so habitat  connectivity is really important  it's one of the major causes for decline  in numerous different species  because habita so species need  they need a lot from their environment  they need food they need water they need  shelter  and they can't get that or they can't  necessarily get that depending on the  species in isolated pockets they need to  be able to  to move whether that's to to forage for  their food  or for other reasons well joining me now  is environment minister  rebecca powell now tell us more about  what kind of projects are getting this  funding and how they've been chosen  the green recovery challenge fund was a  fund set up last year actually the first  40 million pounds was  shared out last year largely to  environmental charities and conservation  groups and organizations  to do work to restore nature and also to  ensure  that jobs were either retained or new  jobs created  green jobs because there's a raft of  opportunities now  as we go and create many more  sustainable projects  for our nature restoration and for that  other side of the coin to climate change  which is  nature and adaptation so it's all about  kick-starting nature  recovery and tackling climate change as  you say and  welcome though that is there's still a  long way to go isn't there i mean the  government's own advisors the climate  change committee has said there's a  vacuum in policy  so when will we hear about those  policies that are going to get us to net  zero  one of the things of course we're  hearing a lot about right now and i've  been responsible for bringing the  environment bill through the house of  commons it's now  uh been going through the lords and it  will gain royal ascent by the autumn  well that is a  landmark piece of legislation that will  set us  on a sustainable footing for the future  and within that  we will be setting targets in air  quality  quality waste and recycling and nature  which will reset the dial  in terms of um we really am your  sustainable future  one of those targets we've said that we  will aim to  halt the decline of species abundance  basically nature  by 2030 that's a groundbreaking  and very bold target no other government  has done anything like this  we've also got of course our next zero  commitment  uh to achieve by 2030 and indeed we were  the first economy to introduce a law  to end our contribution to climate  change by 2050. so  there are a whole range of measures now  are coming into play and commitments  you've talked a lot about the targets  that are set and everyone agrees they're  ambitious  it's the action that we're not getting  is it we we need those policies when are  we going to hear them  those policies we're already working on  all the targets and  all the pilots are running around the  country to uh  to uh inform us of exactly how people  will put all these measures  into practice i've actually just been  out on a farm today in shropshire  meeting a farmer who's already sowing  cover crops he's  he's looking after the health of his  soil he's even still growing maize but  he's growing it through grass so you  don't get the runoff you don't get the  wash off  he's been growing his potatoes with the  whole border of physilius which is a  wonderful  flowering plant for the insects so  already a lot's being done but when we  introduce our new environmental land  management scheme now that we've left  the common agricultural policy we can  have our own policy  to tailor-make it to deliver these  targets  and that is going to be introduced soon  the first  bit of it is the sustainable farming  initiative  and farmers are already signing up for  that so we're already moving in that  direction  and yes there's an awful lot to do but i  truly believe  this government has the policies in  place now  for us to deliver on these targets and  actually we need to do it  okay rebecca powell thank you well  that's everything from us for today  next time what's the environmental  impact of a volcano eruption  you can find out tomorrow here on the  daily climate show see you then  you

